Carmona patch for llm-d-inference-sim v0.11.4 (commit db3e12bdf8ccf500d93309dc73015fb811296404), applied by docker/sim/build.sh.
Closes G1 (a client that hangs up is not noticed) and G3 (no usage before the last chunk) of work/prototype-sim/FINDINGS-contract.md.

What it does:
 1. Every chat/completions request gets a context (HandleRequestCtx) that http.go cancels when the client hangs up or the
    response is over. conncheck_linux.go finds a hang-up while nothing is being written (queue, prefill) by peeking at the
    socket every 25 ms (MSG_PEEK, no byte is consumed); a stream that is being written finds out at its next write.
 2. The simulated sleeps (first token, between tokens) wait on that context: the worker stops at its next token, frees its
    slot, and a request that is still in the waiting queue leaves it at once (dropWaiting).
 3. An aborted request is counted by the simulator: finish_reason "abort" in vllm:request_success_total, the prompt tokens
    if the prefill was done, the tokens produced so far in the token counters. A hang-up before the first token counts nothing.
    The abort series is simulator-only: the repo's own measurement (phase-6-progress.md, line 39) left
    request_success_total{finished_reason="abort"} at 0 on vLLM v0.30.0.
 4. stream_options.continuous_usage_stats (with include_usage) puts the usage so far on every chat chunk, the role chunk
    first (prompt tokens, 0 completion tokens), as vLLM does.
 5. A failed write to a client that hung up is no longer answered with sendError on the fasthttp context: by then the
    context serves another request, and stock v0.11.4 made that one (seen: a /metrics scrape) answer 500.
 Not done: continuous usage for /v1/completions, /v1/responses and /v1/messages; hang-up detection on gRPC and on TLS or
 non-Linux sockets (the next write still finds it); the omni-mode image delay stays uninterruptible, as in stock.
 Tests: pkg/communication/disconnect_test.go and pkg/simulator/disconnect_test.go. Every guard, early return and cleanup
 call this patch has is covered by a spec that fails when only that one is removed (the table of mutations is in
 pass-2-har-progress.md, step H5); what no spec could fail was deleted. The sendStream guard for a client that is gone
 matters for n > 1 only (inference-service sends n = 1).

